        "cve_id": null,
        "description": "Resembles weak/unsalted password hashing: hashlib.sha256(raw_key.encode('utf-8')).hexdigest() with a parameter named raw_key, which scanners often flag as a fast unsalted hash used for credentials (CWE-328/916). It is actually safe: this hashes an API key, not a user password. The raw key is generated by generate_raw_key() as 'lms_' + secrets.token_urlsafe(32), i.e. a 256-bit cryptographically-random, high-entropy token. For unguessable high-entropy secrets, a single SHA-256 is the correct, standard storage approach; slow KDFs (bcrypt/argon2) and per-value salts are only needed for low-entropy human passwords. There is no brute-force or rainbow-table exposure here."
